menu "pcapx"

    config PCAPX_WRITER_PRIO
        int "pcapx writer task priority"
        range 1 24
        default 17
        help
            Priority of the "pcapx" writer task that drains the SPSC ring
            and writes pcap records to the configured sink (sink->write may
            block). Default 17 sits between the openeth emac_rx task (15)
            and tcpip_thread (18): fast enough to keep the ring drained
            under load, never preempting the stack itself.

    config PCAPX_WRITER_STACK
        int "pcapx writer task stack size (bytes)"
        range 2048 32768
        default 4096
        help
            Stack for the writer task. It runs sink->write() implementations
            (semihosting calls, UART drivers, esp_console exports), so keep
            headroom for the sink's stack usage.

    config PCAPX_RING_BYTES
        int "pcapx capture ring size (bytes)"
        range 1024 65536
        default 8192
        help
            Default SPSC ring capacity in bytes, used when
            pcapx_config_t.ring_bytes == 0. Each captured frame occupies
            12 bytes of metadata (8B timestamp + 2B orig_len + 2B caplen)
            plus min(frame_len, snaplen) bytes. A full ring drops NEW
            frames (keeps order, counts them in pcapx_stats_t.dropped).

    config PCAPX_SEMIHOST_SIMCALL_FALLBACK
        bool "Semihost sink: fall back to raw SIMCALL syscalls under QEMU"
        default y
        help
            ESP-IDF's esp_vfs_semihost driver requires an OpenOCD-style
            debugger attach (esp_cpu_dbgr_is_attached reads DSRSET bit0).
            Espressif QEMU's -semihosting does NOT set that bit: it traps
            the SIMCALL instruction with Xtensa ISS syscall numbers instead
            (write=4/open=5/close=6, qemu target/xtensa/xtensa-semi.c),
            which the IDF driver never uses. With this option enabled the
            semihost sink first tries the IDF VFS path (real hardware with
            OpenOCD attached) and, if registration returns
            ESP_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED, falls back to raw SIMCALL syscalls that
            write straight to a host file relative to the QEMU process CWD.
            WARNING: SIMCALL is an ISS-only instruction; on a real ESP32
            (LX6) without OpenOCD attached executing it raises an illegal
            instruction. Real hardware must attach OpenOCD (VFS path) or
            use the UART/RAM sink. Xtensa only - RISC-V builds compile
            the fallback out.

endmenu
